## DeprecatedGraphQLField

**Severity:** warning | **Type:** GraphQL

This check reports a `.graphql` file that uses a schema member marked `@deprecated`. A deprecated member still works — the query executes and returns data — so nothing else in the toolchain reports it, and the code keeps running until the member is removed.

The message is the schema's own `@deprecated(reason:)` text, so it names the replacement platformOS publishes rather than wording of its own. All four deprecation kinds are reported: fields, field arguments, enum values and input object fields.

The check requires an up-to-date local copy of the platformOS GraphQL schema. Run `pos-cli check update-docs` to download the latest schema.

No autofix or suggestion is offered. A successor named in a deprecation reason is rarely a drop-in rename — moving from `models` to `records` also changes argument names, the filter input type and the shape of the result — so a mechanical replacement would produce a query that no longer validates.

## Examples

### &#x2717; Incorrect Code Example (Avoid using this):

```graphql
query {
  models(per_page: 20, filter: { name: { value: "note" } }) {
    results {
      id
    }
  }
}
```

`RootQuery.models` is deprecated. The query runs and returns records, and the only signal is this check.

### &#x2713; Correct Code Example (Use this instead):

```graphql
query {
  records(per_page: 20, filter: { table: { value: "note" } }) {
    results {
      id
    }
  }
}
```

Note that the replacement is not a rename: `records` takes `table` where `models` takes `name`.

## Inline `{% raw %}{% graphql %}{% endraw %}` blocks

This check is GraphQL-typed, so it only sees `.graphql` files. A query written inline in a Liquid template is covered by [DeprecatedGraphQLFieldInline](/developer-guide/platformos-check/checks/deprecated-graphql-field-inline) instead.

## Configuration

The default configuration for this check:

```yaml
DeprecatedGraphQLField:
  enabled: true
  severity: warning
```

## Disabling This Check

This check is safe to disable while you are maintaining code against an older platform version, or during a migration where the deprecated form is still deliberate.

```yaml
DeprecatedGraphQLField:
  enabled: false
```

